Senile Pruritus (Idiopathic Xerotic Pruritus)
Geriatric & Frailty
Intense generalized itching >65 years, Severe diffuse xerosis, Excoriations/lichenification, Absence of primary dermatological rash

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-044
Reason for Visit: Worsening generalized itch complicated by new-onset, painful, weeping sores on the lower legs.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly patient with severe, chronic itching who had recently scratched their legs so deeply that the wounds are now oozing yellow fluid and causing acute pain. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for secondary bacterial impetiginization or spreading cellulitis, requiring immediate antibiotic intervention to prevent systemic sepsis prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I scratched my legs until they bled because they were so dry and itchy, and now the sores are burning and leaking yellow fluid."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a chronic history of intense, generalized, and unrelenting itching over the past few months. They note a complete absence of a primary underlying dermatological rash prior to the onset of the scratching. They suffer from severe diffuse xerosis, describing the skin on their legs as dry, rough, scaly, and finely cracked (asteatotic eczema). Over the past week, the chronic repeated scratching has resulted in deep excoriations, hemorrhagic crusts, and secondary lichenification. Today, they complain of new, acute pain in the lower legs. They describe the sudden development of secondary bacterial impetiginization of the deeply excoriated skin lesions, noting the sores are weeping a golden-yellow fluid. They also report the presence of intensely itchy hyperkeratotic papules (prurigo nodularis) on their arms secondary to chronic picking. They experience severe sleep disruption due to the intense itching.
Functional Status: Mobilizes independently; highly distressed by the pain in their legs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of spreading cellulitis and potential bloodstream infection from the open, weeping wounds.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Uncomfortable, frequently reaching down to rub their lower legs, mildly febrile.
Physical Exam: Integumentary exam reveals severe diffuse xerosis universally. The anterior shins bilaterally demonstrate multiple deep excoriations with classic, golden-yellow honey-colored crusts and weeping purulent exudate, indicative of secondary bacterial impetiginization (Staphylococcus or Streptococcus). The surrounding skin is erythematous and warm, but the erythema does not rapidly extend up the calf (localized infection). Numerous hyperkeratotic, excoriated nodules (prurigo nodularis) are present on the extensor forearms. Crucially, there is an absolute absence of a primary dermatological rash outside of the scratch marks. (An entirely normal comprehensive metabolic panel confirming the absence of an underlying systemic cause is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: The patient's fingernails are observed to be long, jagged, and dirty, heavily contributing to the deep tissue trauma and subsequent infection.
Medication Reconciliation: Only taking simple emollients infrequently, which have failed to prevent the severe xerosis.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Senile Pruritus with secondary bilateral Impetiginization of the lower legs - Status: Acute Infective Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of superficial, weeping, crusting skin lesions, and the bilateral, symmetrical nature of the erythema and pain, rather than a unilateral, deeply swollen, and engorged calf.

Primary Autoimmune Blistering Disease (e.g., Bullous Pemphigoid) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of tense, fluid-filled bullae or primary urticarial plaques; the lesions are explicitly secondary to mechanical trauma (scratching) and bacterial crusting.

Severe Systemic Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient is only mildly febrile, hemodynamically stable, and the infection appears localized to the superficial dermis (impetigo) rather than deep, spreading fascial planes.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Risk of progressive skin infection)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a clear complication of severe, unmanaged senile pruritus: secondary bacterial infection (impetiginization) of deep scratch wounds. Telephone triage correctly identified the transition from a simple itch to a painful, weeping infection. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have allowed the superficial Staphylococcal/Streptococcal infection to rapidly progress to deep cellulitis or systemic bacteremia, particularly given the patient's age and frailty. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the immediate clinical diagnosis of the bacterial infection, the initiation of targeted systemic antibiotics, and the enforcement of crucial topical barrier therapy to treat the underlying cause (xerosis) and prevent recurrence.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced a 7-day course of oral antibiotics targeting skin flora (e.g., cephalexin or flucloxacillin 500mg QID) for the impetiginization. Instructed staff to gently wash the crusts with warm water and apply an antiseptic cream (e.g., mupirocin or hydrogen peroxide cream) to the weeping sores. Prescribed a thick emollient to be applied generously to all intact skin.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a "trim and treat" routine for the nursing staff. Step 1: Staff meticulously trim and file the patient's fingernails to remove all sharp edges, and then apply the thick emollient to the patient's intact skin twice daily. Step 2: Upon successful completion of the fingernail trimming and moisturizing, the staff provides positive reinforcement by giving the patient a small, preferred snack and a soft, clean pair of cotton long pants. Cause-and-effect: Eliminating the mechanical weapon (sharp nails) physically prevents further deep tissue trauma, while the heavy moisturization and soft clothing treat the underlying dry skin and protect the legs, breaking the cycle of itch-scratch-infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed facility staff to monitor the leg erythema closely.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 48-72 hours to assess the response to antibiotics and ensure the erythema is receding.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the red, hot area around the sores begins to spread rapidly up the leg, if the leg becomes intensely swollen and painful, or if the patient develops a high fever with shaking chills (rigors).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical examination confirms a bacterial skin infection secondary to scratching; the underlying cause of the itch is severe dry skin.
Clinical Course: The antibiotics will clear the yellow weeping and redness over the next few days. However, you must stop scratching and aggressively moisturize to prevent this from happening again.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

(Directed to nursing staff): Transfer the patient to the Emergency Department immediately if the redness spreads rapidly up the leg, or if they spike a high fever. The GP must review the legs in 2 to 3 days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your skin became so dry it cracked, causing intense itching. By scratching so deeply with sharp fingernails, you introduced bacteria from your hands into the open wounds, causing the painful, yellow, weeping infection. The antibiotic pills will kill the bacteria, but we must heal the dry skin to stop the itching.
Actionable Steps: Take the antibiotics exactly as prescribed until finished. Allow the nurses to trim your fingernails very short and file them smooth. Stop taking hot showers, and apply the thick ointment heavily every single day.

